a migrant camp out in the open  in one of the most dangerous cities in  mexico  this is reynossa it's known across  mexico as a black hole where several  criminal groups are battling  and even news struggles to get out  it's one of the places where u.s  authorities are sending back people that  they've caught trying to get across the  border  ngos say more arrive every day  in the day we are okay but when the  night comes it's terrifying  reynosa is very dangerous we've heard  shootouts  reynos has endured years of violence  just a month ago  a group of our men drove down the town  streets randomly killing bystanders  karina was actually kidnapped in a  nearby town  i have the scars a lady burnt me but i  didn't tell the police  i just healed myself the best i could i  was held for more than a month and a  half  are more stories like that in the camp  as well as those the u.s has sent back  across the bridge there are also  those who were trying to cross for the  first time through the front door as  asylum  seekers karina told us that its  inhabitants have got organized  now men get together and around 40 of  them patrol the perimeter each night  the conditions are tough basic needs are  hard to meet  kovid's a constant threat  and food mostly comes from donations  the numbers are fluid but there are  around 600 people there currently  ngos and government authorities are  trying to relocate them into shelters  migrants will always be a target  unfortunately they are one of the  vulnerable groups  people that don't have an address or a  home  the problem is that as soon as  authorities and civil society groups  move people into an available space  more arrive either through mexico or  across the bridge from the states  john holman al jazeera mexico
 

